{
  "analysis": "The review describes a chance-based wheel reward mechanic in which the wheel appears to stop on a valuable prize, but the game then redirects it to another spin and delivers a substantially lower reward. The apparent result is therefore manipulated after the initial stop, which supports Illusion of Control. The wheel also uses unpredictable, repeated spins to determine rewards, supporting Reward Mania. No real-money stake is explicitly described, so Gambling is not assigned.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "the wheel spin after stopping on a great price the genie move the wheel to a spin tag, and then the prices after the spin are way lower than the original price",
      "rule_applied": "vs Alleged rigging",
      "rationale": "The span alleges that the game changes or manipulates the wheel's apparent outcome after it has stopped, concealing how the reward is determined."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_RewardMania",
      "span": "the wheel spin after stopping on a great price",
      "rule_applied": "Variable Rewards; chance-based reward delivery",
      "rationale": "The wheel provides unpredictable rewards through repeated spins, making the delivery mechanism itself the source of continued engagement."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review attributes nearly all described behaviors to bugs, crashing, lag, freezing, and unresponsive controls, so those technical malfunctions do not support dark-pattern labels under R11. It explicitly mentions PvP, which is a game mode involving competition against other players; that functional mechanic remains codable despite the reported lag. Opening gifts and trading with friends are mentioned as activities disrupted by crashes, but no obligation to reciprocate or social recruitment pressure is described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "the pvp",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "PvP explicitly describes competition against other players, even though the review says the mode is malfunctioning."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a matchmaking connection wait, but this is a service or matchmaking delay rather than an in-game timer that schedules play, so it does not support Playing by Appointment. It also describes difficulty obtaining weapon upgrades and perks through continued battling, but it does not clearly identify repetitive or tedious activity as the required progression method, so Grinding is not assigned. The references to connecting to a match and battling to win indicate competition against other players. The claim that rewards are merely for show is evaluative and does not name a specific reward-delivery mechanic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "connect to a match",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "Connecting to a match indicates participation in competitive gameplay against other players."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes competitive matches against other players, explicitly through playing against opponents and trying to win. It also explicitly characterizes the game as pay to win and says that advancing higher is not achievable through skill alone, implying that spending provides an advantage or is needed for progression. The complaints about Mega Knight, P.E.K.K.A., and Golem being spammed and difficult to defend describe disliked balance and ordinary competitive strategy, not anti-social behavior or a hidden outcome-manipulation system. The statement that cards are buffed in updates does not show that a purchased item lost value, so Power Creep is not supported.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "all you play against after 8k trophies is mega knight,pekka, and golem",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The reviewer explicitly describes matches played against other players."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "its also pay to win and usually doesn't let you get higher from just using skill",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win indicator under M_PayToProgress",
      "rationale": "The span states that spending money provides an advantage and that advancing cannot usually be achieved through skill alone."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a chance-based game mechanic in which a game element may fail to appear after five original moves and five additional moves, leaving the player disputing a charge or loss of 9 diamonds. This establishes a concrete loss of in-game currency without the expected result, supporting Waste Aversion; it does not clearly establish a wager or a real-money conversion, so Gambling and Intermediate Currency are not assigned. The reference to buying “the pass” describes a paid pass, which supports Recurring Fee. Finally, trading lives with friends is a resource exchange with existing players, supporting Reciprocity.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_WasteAversion",
      "span": "Customer Service can't make good on 9 diamonds when game element doesn't appear within 5 original moves and 5 additional moves",
      "rule_applied": "Waste Aversion boundary rule: assign when the text explicitly demonstrates stranded, forfeited, or insufficient value, including a concrete shortfall or loss.",
      "rationale": "The player spent or lost 9 diamonds but did not receive the expected game element, showing realized wasted in-game value."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_RecurringFee",
      "span": "you don't buy from this game beyond the pass",
      "rule_applied": "M_RecurringFee indicator: battle passes are recurring-fee mechanics.",
      "rationale": "The review explicitly identifies a paid game pass as a purchase option."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Reciprocity",
      "span": "trade lives",
      "rule_applied": "S_Reciprocity indicator: players exchange resources, lives, or favours with other players.",
      "rationale": "Trading lives is an explicit resource exchange with friends and a team."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes two existing time-based mechanics. First, lives regenerate over a real-world timer, as shown by the request for a shorter wait before receiving a new life; this is a Wait to Play mechanic. Second, the game provides daily prizes and resets the player's progress in those prizes when they miss a day, which is a daily-return incentive with a missed-day penalty. The reviewer phrases both as suggestions, but the underlying life timer and daily-prize reset are described as currently present mechanics. The comments about harder levels and unnecessary tasks do not by themselves establish Grinding or another coded pattern.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "maybe only 5-10 minutes for a new life",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play indicator: in-game timers that make the player wait for something; R12 permits coding the existing timer even though the reviewer suggests changing its duration.",
      "rationale": "The span implies that a player must wait for a life to regenerate before continuing to play."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_DailyRewards",
      "span": "just because you miss a day doesnt mean that you need to start over on your daily prizes",
      "rule_applied": "Daily Rewards definition and boundary rule: daily incentives with an active consequence for missing a day, such as a broken streak or reset progress.",
      "rationale": "The game offers daily prizes and resets the player's daily-prize progress when they fail to return on a given day."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an in-game advertisement that cannot be dismissed normally: there is no close control, tapping the screen redirects the player to the store, returning to the game leaves the advertisement displayed, and the player must terminate the game to escape it. This is a forced or effectively unskippable advertisement and an interface that obstructs the opt-out while routing taps toward the store. The review does not attribute the behavior to a bug or technical fault.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "after seeing the ads, there is no way to close it",
      "rule_applied": "Forced to watch ads or given rewards for watching ads",
      "rationale": "The advertisement cannot be dismissed through the normal interface, compelling the player to remain exposed to it."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "No cross mark on the screen , if you try to click anywhere on the screen it takes you to store",
      "rule_applied": "The interface obstructs an opt-out or exit and directs attention or input toward a purchase/store option.",
      "rationale": "The missing close control prevents opting out, while tapping the screen routes the player to the store instead."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes that deleting the game would cause the player to lose accumulated in-game purchases and progress, which makes leaving costly and creates reluctance to uninstall. The request to add saving is hypothetical and is not coded as a separate mechanic; the currently described loss of accumulated value is sufficient for Easy to Get, Hard to Lose.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_EasyToGetHardToLose",
      "span": "if I deleted the game I would lose everything my in-game purchases and my hard work I really don't want that to happen",
      "rule_applied": "Invested (Endowed) Value: accumulated time, money, and progress make abandoning the game difficult; the span shows that leaving would forfeit value already held.",
      "rationale": "The player is reluctant to delete the game because doing so would sacrifice their purchases and hard-earned progress."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes empire-building and expansion, but it does not specify a repetitive progress activity, payment mechanic, timer, reward structure, or other dark-pattern behavior. It explicitly describes battling other players in real-time, which is player-versus-player competition.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "the thrill of battling other players in real-time combat",
      "rule_applied": "definition: The game makes players compete against other players.",
      "rationale": "The span explicitly describes real-time battles between players."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes progression being contingent on purchasing because the game provides no extra energy. This is an explicit paid alternative to advancing. It does not establish a wait timer or energy regeneration schedule, so Playing by Appointment is not assigned, and no specific in-game currency is identified for Intermediate Currency.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "to have any change at progressing you need to purchase",
      "rule_applied": "definition; Free alternative boundary rule",
      "rationale": "The reviewer states that progressing requires a purchase, making payment necessary to advance."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an energy system that limits continued play: once the player's energy is depleted, they cannot meaningfully progress until the energy regenerates, and the recovery timer is long. This is an in-game wait timer that gates progression and requires the player to return according to the game's regeneration schedule. The requests for console play, a storyline, bigger maps, and more customization are hypothetical additions and are not coded.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "It's an Energy based game so once you run out you can't really progress any further, and the timer is fairly long to recover fully.",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play indicator and boundary rule: the span describes an in-game regeneration timer that gates progress.",
      "rationale": "Progress stops when energy is depleted and can resume only after a lengthy real-time recovery period."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes purchased Poké Balls being consumed when Pokémon break out of attempted captures, leading the player to buy more. The reviewer is alleging that capture outcomes are manipulated after purchases so that the player depletes the balls faster; this is a claim that the game determines outcomes in a concealed or engineered way, supporting Illusion of Control. The later edit says the displayed review was not written by the reviewer and falsely claimed conversations with other players, which describes a review being posted under the player's identity by someone else, supporting Friend Spam / Impersonation under the destination-of-message rule. The text does not clearly establish that payment is required for progression, nor that real money is converted into currency or wagered in a gambling mechanic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "since I started purchasing, items \"poke\" balls that when I tried to make a capture that they keep breaking out so I go through the balls faster so I have to purchase more",
      "rule_applied": "Alleged rigging boundary under Illusion of Control",
      "rationale": "The reviewer attributes repeated capture failures and faster ball depletion to a concealed change in outcome determination after purchasing."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_FriendSpamImpersonation",
      "span": "I DID NOT WRITE THIS REVIEW.....I DIDN'T TALK TO SEVERAL PLAYERS BECAUSE I DO NOT KNOW ANYONE",
      "rule_applied": "Destination of the message rule; the game or product is described as posting a review under the player's identity",
      "rationale": "The edit explicitly denies authorship of the review and denies the interactions it reports, implying that a review was posted on the player's behalf."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes progress being blocked by difficult levels and states that some tasks cannot be completed unless the player buys boosters. The week-long difficulty describes slow progress but does not identify an imposed timer, repeated task, or other qualifying temporal mechanic. The paid boosters are presented as necessary to complete otherwise impossible tasks, which directly supports Pay to Progress.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "there are way too many impossible tasks unless you buy boosters",
      "rule_applied": "Free alternative boundary rule",
      "rationale": "The review describes a free progression path that is effectively impossible unless the player pays for boosters."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes a time-limited event with a reward that was nearly obtained, a stated remaining window of two days, and then an event change after the player stopped playing overnight. The event clock and its expiration or replacement determine when the player must play to secure the reward, so this is a schedule imposed by the game. The review does not describe a daily login reward, a purchase, paid currency, gambling, or a resource regeneration timer. Although the player had three steps left and ran out of dice, no explicit wait or dice-refill schedule is given, so that does not independently establish Playing by Appointment through resource depletion.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "The latest event I was about to get 525 dice as reward, I had 3 left to go and I ran out of dice, it said 2Days left. Now just logged in after sleeping and the event is now changed.",
      "rule_applied": "Playing by Appointment boundary rule: where a reward expires on the game's clock and the span describes the schedule or the loss, code Playing by Appointment.",
      "rationale": "The event's game-controlled time window dictated when the player needed to play, and the event changed before the stated two-day period had elapsed, putting the nearly earned reward at risk."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes several game mechanics. It says the game formerly rewarded inviting friends and getting them to join Facebook, which is a social recruitment incentive. It also describes wagering dice through a max-bet mechanic and receiving no favorable outcomes such as Shut Downs or bank heists, establishing a chance-based stake with no real-money purchase mentioned. The repeated duplicate stickers describe an unpredictable reward pool producing unwanted repeated items, which fits Reward Mania. The internet connection error is explicitly attributed to a technical malfunction, so it does not itself receive a dark-pattern label.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_ForcedFellowship",
      "span": "no rewards for inviting friends and getting them to join facebook",
      "rule_applied": "Social Pyramid Scheme indicator",
      "rationale": "The game previously rewarded the player for inviting friends and recruiting them into the game or its social network."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_Gambling",
      "span": "when I finally had more than 500 dices and played max bet, absolutely NO LUCK! No Shut downs and no bank heists.",
      "rule_applied": "Gambling vs Reward Mania boundary: a wager using earned in-game currency qualifies even without a real-money purchase being described.",
      "rationale": "The player wagered accumulated dice at a maximum bet for chance-based outcomes such as Shut Downs or bank heists."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_RewardMania",
      "span": "Stickers are duplicates all the way...",
      "rule_applied": "Variable Rewards indicator; repeated unwanted drops from a randomized reward pool",
      "rationale": "Repeated duplicate stickers imply a variable reward system that requires repeated attempts while often delivering unwanted items."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an interface that automatically scrolls to and selects the highest-bet table when the game opens or when the player enters the 1v1 section. This selection is positioned to cause accidental mis-clicks, making players lose their accumulated coins. The reviewer also states that this loss forces players to purchase more coins with real money. The automatic selection and mis-click risk support UI Misdirection. The conversion of real money into coins supports Intermediate Currency, and the stated need to buy coins to continue playing supports Pay to Progress. The review does not explicitly establish a chance-based wager, so Gambling is not assigned.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "it automatically scrolls and selects the highest possible bet table based on your coins",
      "rule_applied": "Attention-directing without an opt-out; UI Misdirection applies where interface placement or automatic selection directs the player toward a costly option and increases the risk of a mis-tap.",
      "rationale": "The interface automatically directs the player to the highest-bet table rather than a neutral or lowest-bet option, creating the described accidental mis-click risk."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_IntermediateCurrency",
      "span": "forced to buy real ones",
      "rule_applied": "Intermediate Currency definition: real-world money is used to acquire in-game currency for use within the game world.",
      "rationale": "The phrase implies that players purchase more in-game coins with real money after losing their existing coins."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "players lose all their hard-earned coins and are forced to buy real ones",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Progress definition and boundary rule: payment restores access or bypasses an artificial barrier that blocks continued play or advancement.",
      "rationale": "The interface-induced loss of coins creates a barrier that the review says players must overcome by purchasing more coins to keep playing."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a random-number system that the player believes is biased against them, asserting that the game manipulates chance outcomes rather than merely reporting an unlucky run. It also contrasts playing without real-money spending with the paid route and says that, around level 100, completing a level costs 2200 while a win barely returns that amount, implying that advancement becomes economically unfavorable without spending. The review does not describe unpredictable reward delivery, a collection goal, a timer, or a direct real-money-to-currency conversion.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "the rng is so obviously against you",
      "rule_applied": "Illusion of Control boundary rule vs alleged rigging",
      "rationale": "The reviewer asserts that the game's random outcome system is manipulated against the player, rather than describing an ordinary losing streak."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "it's not rewarding playing the game without spending actual money",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Progress boundary rule vs free alternative",
      "rationale": "The span contrasts the free route with spending actual money and implies that continued advancement is not viable without payment."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a base-building game with events in which winning is portrayed as requiring substantial real-money spending. “Pay to play” and “pay to win,” together with spending 30k to win the last event, indicate that payment provides a competitive advantage and is tied to success in the event. The event itself establishes player-versus-player or leaderboard-style competition. “Same old same old grinder” is only a general characterization of repetition, without naming a repeated task required for progress, so it does not satisfy Grinding.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "Zero skill pay to play. Zero skill pay to win. Calculated total spent on last event was 30k to win.",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win indicator: patterns that grant advantages to players who spend money.",
      "rationale": "The span explicitly links real-money spending to winning an event, indicating that payment provides an advantage needed for success."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "there is absolutely no opportunity for a general player to win an event",
      "rule_applied": "Competition definition: patterns that make players compete against other players.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer describes winning an event against other participants, establishing a competitive game mechanic."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes several active game mechanics. It explicitly presents spending as a route to competitive advantage, which supports Pay to Progress. Base updates are controlled by increasingly long timers, imposing waits on advancement and supporting Playing by Appointment. The statement that everything scales endlessly and offers only more grind describes an unattainable or continually receding end state, supporting Infinite Treadmill; it does not identify a specific repetitive task, so Grinding is not assigned. The UI is said to be deliberately confusing in order to drive purchases, which directly supports UI Misdirection. Alliances and attacks create player-versus-player conflict, supporting Competition. The review does not describe rewards for bullying, recruitment incentives, or an obligation to reciprocate with allies, so the other social labels are not supported.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "it's set up in a pay to win way",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win indicator under M_PayToProgress",
      "rationale": "The review states that spending money provides an in-game competitive advantage."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "the update times for your base are becoming ridiculous",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play indicator: in-game timers impose arbitrary waits on progress",
      "rationale": "Base advancement is controlled by increasingly long update timers, requiring the player to wait according to the game's schedule."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_InfiniteTreadmill",
      "span": "Everything scales endlessly",
      "rule_applied": "Definition: patterns that continually expand the game to never allow players to complete",
      "rationale": "The game is described as endlessly scaling, implying that progression continually recedes without an attainable completion point."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "The UI is deliberately confusing and overwhelming to achieve the effect that you need to buy stuff",
      "rule_applied": "UI Misdirection boundary rule: the interface is designed to focus or confuse players toward purchasing",
      "rationale": "The reviewer explicitly attributes confusing UI design to steering players toward buying."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "If you are not in one then you get attacked and bullied",
      "rule_applied": "Definition: patterns that make players compete against other players",
      "rationale": "The game places players in hostile player-versus-player interactions where those outside top alliances are attacked."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a daily-reward system whose rewards cannot be completed unless the player places a 50,000 bet. This is a daily return incentive with a condition attached, so Daily Rewards applies. The required bet is also a chance-based wagering mechanic using in-game currency; no real-money purchase of that currency is described, so Gambling applies without Intermediate Currency. The comparison between 50,000 required and 40,000 obtainable from timed rewards describes a shortfall, but not a currency amount stranded after a purchase or an item-cost mismatch, so Waste Aversion does not apply.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_DailyRewards",
      "span": "I can't get my daily rewards unless I bet 50,000 with out it I can't complete my daily rewards",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The game offers daily rewards but requires the player to complete a daily condition to obtain them."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_Gambling",
      "span": "unless I bet 50,000",
      "rule_applied": "Gambling indicator: a chance-based stake using earned in-game currency qualifies even when no real-money purchase is described.",
      "rationale": "The player must wager in-game currency through a betting mechanic to pursue the reward."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes monetized progression and sticker collection. The reviewer says winning anything costs hundreds of dollars, and specifically states that completing the final one or two stickers requires paying a large amount, making payment the route to completion. The sticker goal is a collection objective, so the review also supports a collection-completion mechanic. The final stickers are portrayed as deliberately unavailable through ordinary play and obtainable through expensive payment, which implies manufactured scarcity tied to purchase. The statements about completing a goal only one time out of one hundred suggest poor odds, but they do not clearly identify a chance-based reward mechanism or wager, so Gambling and Reward Mania are not assigned.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "on the stickers you will never get the last one or two unless you pay a lot of money to get them",
      "rule_applied": "Pay Wall / Free alternative",
      "rationale": "Completion of the sticker objective is described as unavailable through ordinary play unless the player pays substantially."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_CompleteTheCollection",
      "span": "you will never get the last one or two",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The reviewer is pursuing the final missing items in a sticker collection."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_DeceptiveLuxury",
      "span": "you will never get the last one or two unless you pay a lot of money to get them",
      "rule_applied": "Artificial Scarcity / Rarity Level",
      "rationale": "The final stickers are described as artificially scarce and tied to an expensive purchase route."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

